[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JavaScript Promises and Async/Await"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Asynchronous JavaScript is crucial for handling operations that take time, such as network requests or file system access, without blocking the main thread. Promises and async/await are modern JavaScript features that simplify asynchronous programming, making it more readable and maintainable."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Promises"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A Promise is an object representing the eventual completion (or failure) of an asynchronous operation. It has three states: pending, fulfilled, or rejected."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Promise"},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"const myPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  // Asynchronous operation here
  setTimeout(() => {
    const success = true; // Or false for rejection
    if (success) {
      resolve('Operation completed successfully!');
    } else {
      reject('Operation failed!');
    }
  }, 1000);
});"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Promise constructor takes a function with two arguments: 'resolve' and 'reject'. 'resolve' is called when the operation succeeds, and 'reject' is called when it fails."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Consuming a Promise"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"myPromise
  .then((result) => {
    console.log('Success:', result);
  })
  .catch((error) => {
    console.error('Error:', error);
  })
  .finally(() => {
    console.log('Promise finished.');
  });"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The '.then()' method is called when the promise is resolved, '.catch()' is called when it's rejected, and '.finally()' is called regardless of the outcome."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Promise Chaining"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Promises can be chained together to perform a sequence of asynchronous operations."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const promise1 = Promise.resolve(10);

promise1
  .then((value) => {
    console.log('First then:', value);
    return value * 2;
  })
  .then((value) => {
    console.log('Second then:', value);
    return value + 5;
  })
  .then((value) => {
    console.log('Third then:', value);
  });"},{"order":14,"type":"heading","text":"Async/Await"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Async/await is syntactic sugar built on top of promises, making asynchronous code look and behave a bit more like synchronous code. It makes asynchronous code easier to read and write."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Async Functions"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"An async function is declared with the 'async' keyword. It implicitly returns a Promise."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"async function myFunction() {
  return 'Hello, async!';
}

myFunction().then(console.log);"},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Await Keyword"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'await' keyword can only be used inside an async function. It pauses the execution of the function until the promise is resolved or rejected."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"async function fetchData() {
  try {
    const response = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1');
    const data = await response.json();
    console.log(data);
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
  }
}

fetchData();"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'await fetch(...)' pauses the execution until the fetch promise resolves. Similarly, 'await response.json()' waits for the JSON parsing to complete. Error handling is done using a try/catch block."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Error Handling"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"With async/await, you can use standard try/catch blocks for error handling, making it more straightforward than using '.catch()' with promises."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"async function processData() {
  try {
    const result = await someAsyncFunction();
    console.log('Result:', result);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('An error occurred:', error);
  }
}"},{"order":26,"type":"heading","text":"Benefits of Promises and Async/Await"},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved code readability and maintainability."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simplified asynchronous error handling."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Easier to reason about asynchronous code flow."},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"By using Promises and async/await, you can write cleaner, more efficient, and easier-to-understand asynchronous JavaScript code."}]
